{"id":1968,"date":"2020-12-14T09:16:59","date_gmt":"2020-12-14T17:16:59","guid":{"rendered":"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/?post_type=lesson&#038;p=1968"},"modified":"2024-09-01T13:58:05","modified_gmt":"2024-09-01T20:58:05","slug":"zumi-python-gyroscope","status":"publish","type":"lesson","link":"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/lesson\/zumi-python-gyroscope\/","title":{"rendered":"1.6: Gyroscope"},"content":{"rendered":"<div class=\"wpb-content-wrapper\"><p>[vc_row][vc_column]<a id=\"6a2215182dd35\" name=\"6a2215182dd35\" class=\"scroll-spy-anchor\"><\/a>\n<div class=\"robolink-step \">\n\n\t\t<div class=\"robolink-step-head\"><span>Step 1<\/span><\/div>\n\t\n\t\t\t<h2>Gyroscope<\/h2>\n\t\n\t<p>[vc_column_text]When you think of a gyroscope, you may be thinking of a mounted, spinning wheel that stays balanced in the same orientation, no matter which direction you turn or flip the gyroscope. How? This can be explained by conservation of momentum. While you may not have heard of this term before, you have experienced this. For example, if you have ever spun around on an office chair, you probably noticed you spin faster when you bring your arms and legs in. Since momentum has to be conserved, your speed increases when you bring your mass (arms and legs) closer to you.[\/vc_column_text][vc_single_image image=&#8221;1971&#8243; img_size=&#8221;large&#8221; alignment=&#8221;center&#8221;][vc_column_text]In electronics, gyroscopes don&#8217;t look like this. They are tiny chips that also use motion to detect changes in orientation. For robotics, this device measures rotation speed. Gyroscopes are important because we need rotation speed to calculate how many degrees Zumi has turned in a given amount of time.[\/vc_column_text]<\/p>\n<\/div>\n[\/vc_column][\/vc_row][vc_row][vc_column]<a id=\"6a2215182de6c\" name=\"6a2215182de6c\" class=\"scroll-spy-anchor\"><\/a>\n<div class=\"robolink-step \">\n\n\t\t<div class=\"robolink-step-head\"><span>Step 2<\/span><\/div>\n\t\n\t\t\t<h2>Import libraries<\/h2>\n\t\n\t<p>[vc_column_text]In [ ]:<\/p>\n<pre class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"python\">from zumi.zumi import Zumi\r\nfrom zumi.util.screen import Screen\r\nimport time\r\n\r\nzumi = Zumi()\r\nscreen = Screen()<\/pre>\n<p>[\/vc_column_text]<\/p>\n<\/div>\n[\/vc_column][\/vc_row][vc_row][vc_column]<a id=\"6a2215182dfde\" name=\"6a2215182dfde\" class=\"scroll-spy-anchor\"><\/a>\n<div class=\"robolink-step \">\n\n\t\t<div class=\"robolink-step-head\"><span>Step 3<\/span><\/div>\n\t\n\t\t\t<h2>Axes<\/h2>\n\t\n\t<p>[vc_column_text]There is more than one axis that you can use to measure rotational speed. The axis you will be most concerned with is yaw, or measuring turns to the left and right. You can also measure if Zumi is tilting forward and backward or tilting left and right. These three axes are called roll, pitch, and yaw.[\/vc_column_text][vc_single_image image=&#8221;1972&#8243; img_size=&#8221;large&#8221; alignment=&#8221;center&#8221;][vc_column_text]There are three codes below reading all three axes: X, Y, and Z. Run each one and check Zumi&#8217;s screen to see how the angles are affected. Can you match X, Y, and Z with roll, pitch, and yaw? Make sure you start the code with Zumi flat on the ground before picking her up.<\/p>\n<h3 id=\"X-angle\">X-angle<\/h3>\n<p>In [ ]:<\/p>\n<pre class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"python\">zumi.reset_gyro()\r\nfor i in range(0,50):\r\n    current_angle = int(zumi.read_x_angle())\r\n    message = \" X-Angle reading           \"\r\n    message = message + str(current_angle)\r\n    screen.draw_text(message)\r\n    time.sleep(0.05)\r\n    \r\nprint(\"Done\")\r\nscreen.draw_text_center(\"Done\")<\/pre>\n<h3 id=\"Y-angle\">Y-angle<\/h3>\n<pre class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"python\">zumi.reset_gyro()\r\nfor i in range(0,50):\r\n    current_angle = int(zumi.read_y_angle())\r\n    message = \" Y-Angle reading           \"\r\n    message = message + str(current_angle)\r\n    screen.draw_text(message)\r\n    time.sleep(0.05)\r\n    \r\nprint(\"Done\")\r\nscreen.draw_text_center(\"Done\")<\/pre>\n<h3 id=\"Z-angle\">Z-angle<\/h3>\n<pre class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"python\">zumi.reset_gyro()\r\nfor i in range(0,50):\r\n    current_angle = int(zumi.read_z_angle())\r\n    message = \" Z-Angle reading           \"\r\n    message = message + str(current_angle)\r\n    screen.draw_text(message)\r\n    time.sleep(0.05)\r\n    \r\nprint(\"Done\")\r\nscreen.draw_text_center(\"Done\")<\/pre>\n<p>Based on the data, could you figure out which directions correspond to X, Y, and Z?[\/vc_column_text][vc_single_image image=&#8221;1973&#8243; img_size=&#8221;large&#8221; alignment=&#8221;center&#8221;][vc_column_text]For the purposes of driving, you will care the most about the Z-axis, or yaw, of Zumi&#8217;s gyroscope. Calling\u00a0<span style=\"font-family: Courier;\">zumi.read_z_angle()<\/span>\u00a0will return the number of degrees you have turned from when you started your code. You will be using this in later programs.[\/vc_column_text]<\/p>\n<\/div>\n[\/vc_column][\/vc_row][vc_row][vc_column]<a id=\"6a2215182e057\" name=\"6a2215182e057\" class=\"scroll-spy-anchor\"><\/a>\n<div class=\"robolink-step \">\n\n\t\t<div class=\"robolink-step-head\"><span>Step 4<\/span><\/div>\n\t\n\t\t\t<h2>Reset Gyroscope<\/h2>\n\t\n\t<p>[vc_column_text]You may have noticed a function at the beginning of each code cell\u00a0<span style=\"font-family: Courier;\">reset_gyro()<\/span>. This function will set all angles for roll, pitch, and yaw back to zero. Running\u00a0<span style=\"font-family: Courier;\">zumi=Zumi()<\/span>\u00a0will always do this for you, but if you ever need to reset your angles later in your program you can use this function.<\/p>\n<p>In [ ]:<\/p>\n<pre class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"python\">zumi.reset_gyro() \r\nfor i in range(100):\r\n    z_angle = int(zumi.read_z_angle()) # &lt;-- This function reads the angle\r\n    message = \" Z Angle reading        \"\r\n    message = message + str(z_angle)\r\n    screen.draw_text(message)\r\n    time.sleep(0.1)<\/pre>\n<p>[\/vc_column_text]<\/p>\n<\/div>\n[\/vc_column][\/vc_row][vc_row][vc_column]<a id=\"6a2215182e0ba\" name=\"6a2215182e0ba\" class=\"scroll-spy-anchor\"><\/a>\n<div class=\"robolink-step \">\n\n\t\t<div class=\"robolink-step-head\"><span>Step 5<\/span><\/div>\n\t\n\t\t\t<h2>Absolute Angles<\/h2>\n\t\n\t<p>[vc_column_text]In the previous lesson, you made shapes by using\u00a0<strong>relative<\/strong>\u00a0angles. This means that when you call\u00a0<span style=\"font-family: Courier;\">zumi.turn_left(30)<\/span>, for example, Zumi will turn 30 degrees no matter which direction she is currently facing. However, this is not a good way to keep track of our\u00a0<strong>heading<\/strong>, or the direction we are pointing. Think about how we use North, South, East, and West to keep ourselves from getting lost.[\/vc_column_text][vc_single_image image=&#8221;1975&#8243; img_size=&#8221;large&#8221; alignment=&#8221;center&#8221;][vc_column_text]In the previous section you learned that when you call the Zumi object or run\u00a0<span style=\"font-family: Courier;\">reset_gyro()<\/span>, Zumi will reset all gyroscope values to zero. Think of this action as resetting your &#8220;North&#8221;.[\/vc_column_text]<\/p>\n<\/div>\n[\/vc_column][\/vc_row][vc_row][vc_column]<a id=\"6a2215182e118\" name=\"6a2215182e118\" class=\"scroll-spy-anchor\"><\/a>\n<div class=\"robolink-step \">\n\n\t\t<div class=\"robolink-step-head\"><span>Step 6<\/span><\/div>\n\t\n\t\t\t<h2>zumi.turn()<\/h2>\n\t\n\t<p>[vc_column_text]You learned about\u00a0<span style=\"font-family: Courier;\">zumi.turn_left()<\/span>\u00a0and\u00a0<span style=\"font-family: Courier;\">zumi.turn_right()<\/span>, but now it&#8217;s time to introduce a new function. To use absolute angles, call\u00a0<span style=\"font-family: Courier;\">zumi.turn()<\/span>. See the diagram below to understand how this works:[\/vc_column_text][vc_single_image image=&#8221;1976&#8243; img_size=&#8221;large&#8221; alignment=&#8221;center&#8221;][vc_column_text]In this case, 0 degrees is North, 90 degrees is West, 180 degrees is South, and 270 degrees is East. Similarly, -90 degrees is East, -180 degrees is South, and -270 degrees is West. You would use the positive values to turn to the left and negative values to turn to the right.[\/vc_column_text]<\/p>\n<\/div>\n[\/vc_column][\/vc_row][vc_row][vc_column]<a id=\"6a2215182e18d\" name=\"6a2215182e18d\" class=\"scroll-spy-anchor\"><\/a>\n<div class=\"robolink-step \">\n\n\t\t<div class=\"robolink-step-head\"><span>Step 7<\/span><\/div>\n\t\n\t\t\t<h2>Squares with heading<\/h2>\n\t\n\t<p>[vc_column_text]<span style=\"font-size: medium;\">Now with your new knowledge about using absolute heading as you would use a compass to get around the neighborhood, write code for driving in a square that uses the\u00a0<span style=\"font-family: Courier;\">turn()<\/span>\u00a0function. Imagine you are giving Zumi directions in this way:<\/span><\/p>\n<p><span style=\"font-family: Courier;\">Go North for 1 block<br \/>\nGo West for 1 block<br \/>\nGo South for 1 block<br \/>\nGo East for 1 block<br \/>\n<\/span><\/p>\n<p><span style=\"font-size: medium;\">Use the images above to help you. Hint: Start by going forward then changing your heading by using\u00a0<span style=\"font-family: Courier;\">turn()<\/span>.<\/span>[\/vc_column_text]<\/p>\n<\/div>\n[\/vc_column][\/vc_row]<\/p>\n<\/div>","protected":false},"excerpt":{"rendered":"[vc_row][vc_column] Step 8 Gyroscope [vc_column_text]When you think of a gyroscope, you may be thinking of a mounted, spinning wheel that stays balanced in the same orientation, no matter which direction&nbsp;<a href=\"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/lesson\/zumi-python-gyroscope\/\">&hellip;<\/a>","protected":false},"featured_media":2050,"template":"","meta":{"_initial_content":"","_new_post":false,"_quiz_has_questions":false,"_lesson_complexity":"","_lesson_length":10,"_lesson_course":810,"_lesson_preview":"preview"},"lesson-category":[],"lesson-tag":[2135,2161,2122,2134,2189,2230],"class_list":["post-1968","lesson","type-lesson","status-publish","has-post-thumbnail","hentry","lesson-tag-beginner","lesson-tag-gyroscope","lesson-tag-programming","lesson-tag-python","lesson-tag-sensors","lesson-tag-zumi","module-beginner","post","odd"],"_links":{"self":[{"href":"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/wp-json\/wp\/v2\/lessons\/1968","targetHints":{"allow":["GET"]}}],"collection":[{"href":"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/wp-json\/wp\/v2\/lessons"}],"about":[{"href":"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/wp-json\/wp\/v2\/types\/lesson"}],"version-history":[{"count":6,"href":"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/wp-json\/wp\/v2\/lessons\/1968\/revisions"}],"predecessor-version":[{"id":9143,"href":"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/wp-json\/wp\/v2\/lessons\/1968\/revisions\/9143"}],"wp:featuredmedia":[{"embeddable":true,"href":"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/wp-json\/wp\/v2\/media\/2050"}],"wp:attachment":[{"href":"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/wp-json\/wp\/v2\/media?parent=1968"}],"wp:term":[{"taxonomy":"lesson-category","embeddable":true,"href":"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/wp-json\/wp\/v2\/lesson-category?post=1968"},{"taxonomy":"lesson-tag","embeddable":true,"href":"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/wp-json\/wp\/v2\/lesson-tag?post=1968"}],"curies":[{"name":"wp","href":"https:\/\/api.w.org\/{rel}","templated":true}]}}